    thinking: "**Deciphering the Problem**\n\nI\'m currently breaking down the user\'s issue: they\'re unable to push changes to their remote GitHub repository. I\'ve noted they imported the app, made edits with AI Studio, and now face a push blockage. My focus is on the crucial step of isolating the potential cause of the \"can\'t push\" error, looking at common issues that could block this.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Workflow**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a clearer picture now. AI Studio doesn\'t automatically handle `git push` directly from its backend; that\'s the core issue. I\'ve confirmed the agent lacks user credentials for this. The \"export to GitHub\" option within the settings menu is the intended flow. So, the focus is explaining this UI-based export method. I\'m moving toward providing explicit instructions.\n\n\n**Formulating the Method**\n\nNow, I\'m composing explicit instructions for the user. I\'m focusing on the *why* of the issue: I\'ve explained that my cloud environment doesn\'t store their GitHub credentials, making a direct `git push` impossible for security. I\'m currently drafting the steps in Japanese to guide them through the platform-supported export feature, specifically the \"GitHub\343\201\270\343\202\250\343\202\257\343\202\271\343\203\235\343\203\274\343\203\210\" option, which they should use to push changes.\n\n\n**Outlining the Solution**\n\nI\'m now detailing the exact steps. I\'ve broken down the necessary procedure into concise, numbered steps, starting with opening the settings menu, and going through exporting to GitHub. I\'m also addressing a potential backup, offering a \"download as ZIP\" alternative as a secondary workaround should the direct export fail. Next, I am using a professional tone, without jargon, while ensuring clarity on the system constraint and the UI-based workaround. Finally, I\'ll provide this as a Japanese explanation.\n\n```\nAI Studio\343\201\256\347\222\260\345\242\203\345\206\205\343\201\213\343\202\211\343\201